    {
      "type": "security-audit",
      "status": "PASS",
      "started_at": "2026-04-15T10:00:53Z",
      "completed_at": "2026-04-15T10:02:23Z",
      "duration_ms": 90000,
      "summary": [
        "Reviewed 5 files (workspaces.ts, index.ts, app-launcher.ts, error-log.manager.ts, router.ts). All OWASP Top 10 categories evaluated. 0 Critical, 0 High findings. 2 Medium observations recorded (path traversal defence could be hardened with explicit normalisation; error message disclosure in 500 paths). Security sign-off: PASS."
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "security_issues": 0
      },
      "comments": [
        {
          "type": "risk",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-04-15T10:02:23Z",
          "note": "A03/A01 — Path Traversal (defence-in-depth gap): workspaces.ts lines 403 and 462. Both new handlers construct filesystem paths by joining `appConfig.projectsFolder`, `projectId`, `workspaceId`, and (for github-desktop) `repoId` via `path.join()`. These values originate from URL parameters extracted by the router. The router's `extractParams()` captures raw URL path segments without percent-decoding or normalisation — a segment value of `..` would survive extraction. For the github-desktop handler, `project.Repositories.includes(repoId)` provides the primary guard (the repository must be in the project's allow-list), so traversal via `repoId` is effectively blocked as long as ID values are validated at creation time (confirmed by QA). For `projectId` and `workspaceId`, there is no explicit traversal check in these two new handlers; traversal is only blocked indirectly by `workspaceManager.getById()` / `projectManager.getById()` lookup failures — callers can only reach a path if the manager returns a valid record for those IDs. This layered defence is adequate for the current localhost-only threat model, but adding explicit `path.resolve()` + `startsWith(appConfig.projectsFolder)` assertions would remove all doubt and future-proof the code if the server ever moves to a networked deployment. Remediation: add a guard such as `if (!resolvedPath.startsWith(path.resolve(appConfig.projectsFolder))) { sendError(res, 400, 'Invalid path.'); return; }` after constructing `wsFilePath` (line 403) and `repoDir` (line 462)."
        },
        {
          "type": "risk",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-04-15T10:02:23Z",
          "note": "A05 — Error Message Information Disclosure: workspaces.ts lines 413 and 472. On launch failure, the raw `err.message` from `launchApplication` is forwarded directly to the HTTP response body (500) AND to the error log. The launcher error messages include the executable name (e.g. `Failed to launch application \"code\": spawn code ENOENT`). In a multi-user or network-accessible environment this would disclose internal path structure. In the current threat model (localhost single-user CLI tool, server binds to 127.0.0.1 per index.ts:176) this is low practical risk, but is worth noting. Remediation: consider mapping internal error messages to a generic user-facing message (e.g. `'Failed to launch VS Code. Ensure VS Code is installed and available on PATH.'`) while preserving the full message only in the error log entry."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-04-15T10:02:23Z",
          "note": "A09 — Security Logging Coverage: Both new handlers log errors to ErrorLogManager on 500, with well-structured fields (Source, Operation, Severity, Context including ProjectId/WorkspaceId/RepositoryId). No gaps identified. Logging is absent for 400/404 responses, consistent with the existing codebase pattern — these are client errors and do not represent security events in this context."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-04-15T10:02:23Z",
          "note": "A07 — Authentication/Authorization: These endpoints perform no authentication or session checks — consistent with the rest of the codebase. The server binds exclusively to 127.0.0.1 (index.ts line 176), restricting access to the local machine. No auth gap introduced by the new endpoints."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-04-15T10:02:23Z",
          "note": "A06 — Dependencies: No new third-party dependencies were introduced. The implementation uses only Node.js built-ins (`node:child_process`, `node:fs`, `node:path`) and existing internal modules. No CVE exposure added."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-04-15T10:02:23Z",
          "note": "A08 — Software & Data Integrity / Command Injection: app-launcher.ts uses `spawn()` with `shell: false` on non-Windows platforms, which prevents shell injection via the args array. On Windows, `shell: true` is used for `.cmd` launcher compatibility — the `command` argument is a hardcoded string literal ('code' or 'github') supplied by the route handler, not user-controlled input, so shell injection is not a risk in practice."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-04-15T10:02:23Z",
          "note": "Full OWASP Top 10 review summary: A01 (Access Control) — workspace/project membership validated before filesystem access; A02 (Crypto) — no cryptographic operations introduced; A03 (Injection) — spawn args are derived from validated IDs + config paths, not raw user input; A04 (Insecure Design) — guard ordering is correct (entity existence before disk check before launch); A05 (Misconfiguration) — stack traces not leaked, generic 500 messages used except for launcher errors (noted above); A06 (Components) — no new deps; A07 (Auth) — localhost-only server, no regression; A08 (Integrity) — no unsafe deserialization or unsigned updates; A09 (Logging) — error log entries correctly structured; A10 (SSRF) — no outbound HTTP requests; no server-side URL fetching introduced."
        }
      ]
    },
